    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "TWA-Trans World Airlines\nL-049 Constellation\nSitting resplendent in the Arizona sun is the oldest surviving\nConstellation in the world. The tenth airframe to move down\nthe Lockheed production line at Burbank, California, she was\noriginally built for the U.S. Army as a Model C-69-5-LO, and\ndelivered in 1945. IWA purchased the airplane in 1948.\nChristened \"Star of Switzerland\" and modified to an 049\nmodel, Fleet Number 517 accumulated over 37,000 flying\nhours before being sold in 1961. After being operated by\neveral the Pirate Museum atTen and placed red\nmanent display, following renovation by a team of volunteer\nTWA employees who gave over 7000 hours of their time to\nrestore the plane's original TWA colors. Ship 517 is now a\nmain attraction at the museum, located south of the city,\nnear Davis-Monthan Air Force Base.\nPublished by Aviation World,\nInc., P.O. Box 188,\nBethel, Conn. 06801 U.S.A.\nL-016\n75206-D\npost card\nN90831 c/n 1970\n2002.113.459"
    ],
